\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A nonuniform law on sixteen cycle configurations}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{Use the four vertices #{V=\{0,1,2,3\}} of the
[alternating cycle](fdip-0003), the alphabet #{S=\{0,1\}}, and retained
vertices #{J=\{0,2\}}. There are sixteen configurations
#{\Omega=S^V} and four retained configurations #{\Lambda=S^J}.
The map #{\rho(\sigma)=\sigma|_J} is surjective: assign zero at vertices
#{1,3} to extend any retained configuration. Each fiber has four elements.
The [pullback](fdip-0028) identifies retained observables with
those depending only on the values at vertices #{0,2}.}

\p{Choose the following real weights, independently of the edge energies:
##{p(\sigma)=\begin{cases}
1/12,&\sigma_0=\sigma_2,\\
1/24,&\sigma_0\ne\sigma_2.
\end{cases}}
There are eight configurations of each kind, so
#{\sum_\sigma p(\sigma)=8/12+8/24=1}. By the
[finite-state criterion](fdip-0026), this defines a real algebraic
state. Summing the four weights in each fiber gives
##{q(0,0)=q(1,1)=1/3,\qquad q(0,1)=q(1,0)=1/6.}
For #{F(\eta)=\eta_0\eta_2}, only #{\eta=(1,1)} contributes, and hence
##{\omega_q(F)=1/3=4\cdot(1/12)=\omega_p(F\circ\rho).}
This is an explicit instance of the finite fiber-sum identity.}

\p{The law here is a chosen [finite probability distribution](ftip-000A).
It is not obtained from the cycle's edge weights or its precision matrix,
and the carrier #{S^V} is distinct from the real configuration space
#{\mathbb R^V} used by the Gaussian law. The algebra convention remains
\citet{Section 22.2, Tag 09JF}{stacks2017stacks}; the positivity convention
is \citet{Definition 3.9, p. 11}{waldmann2004states}.
If the vertex set is empty, #{S^\varnothing} has one outcome, even for
empty #{S}. An empty alphabet with nonempty vertex set instead gives no
outcomes and no real state. Thus zero vertices and an empty configuration
set are different cases.}
